{"parentUuid":"29175d0f-7897-4f28-bafb-03c6a1570076","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0cbd94e5da2d662d","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- analyze-position\n- cee-cli: Hard policy and failure-handling protocol for driving CEE via `gns cee`. Loads when invoking gns cee or interpreting its output. Forbids workarounds; mandates STOP-and-report on any semantic anomaly.\n- check-cicd: Check CI/CD status for a branch — its PR (with merge queue failures) when one exists, otherwise the workflow runs at the branch's current commit (e.g. master post-merge). Supports /iterate integration.\n- create-or-update-skill: Create, audit, update, or publish a Claude Code skill. ALWAYS use this skill whenever you create, make, write, scaffold, modify, edit, fix, generate, audit, review, or publish any skill — any change to a SKILL.md or run.sh, for new skills and edits to existing ones alike — never hand-edit skill files directly. Use it to scaffold a new skill from scratch (new), audit an existing skill against the authoring conventions read-only (audit), apply fixes to an existing skill (update), and publish or promote a skill to GNS so other machines can install it (--publish / --promote-to-gns). A team or org publish is also advertised in the current repository's AGENTS.md published-skills registry so teammates can discover and install it. Created and updated skills also get a concurrent check of their related skills across GNS for changes published since the last check, surfacing an option to update your version (--check-for-updates). Carries a Specializations registry pointing each skill family to its own authoring-conventions doc (currently: the analyze-position-* family).\n- create-or-update-workspace: Create, update, merge, close, re-open, send to, or inspect editor workspaces (isolated git worktree-plus-branch dev environments where work happens). Use this skill ANY time the user wants to generate, spin up, create, or make a new workspace or branch for some piece of work (optionally off master), or refers to \"the generated workspace\" or \"the workspace generation command\" — that is this skill, never a hand-rolled git worktree or a bare subagent. Also use it to send a follow-up prompt to an existing workspace, merge a workspace back into its source (the \"merge this workspace\" / SPC TAB M action), close or discard a workspace without merging, re-open a previously closed or nuked workspace whose worktree still exists, hand a data payload to a workspace, or check the live status of peer workspaces. One verb per call — create, prompt, merge, close, open, send, or status. Also handles the legacy \"/workspace <verb>\", \"/workspace-generation\", \"/workspace-open\", and \"one shot\"/\"--one-shot\" requests.\n- generate-system-graph\n- gns-sockets\n- render-system-graph\n- show-chess-game\n- sta